Zhang et al.304 prepared a porous $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanorod catalyst using Ce-MOF as a sacrificial template. The preparation involved several sequential steps: first, the authors synthesized the Ce-MOF, followed by anchoring Au onto the Ce-MOF with a chloroauric acid solution. Afterward, they deposited $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ particles on Ce-MOF/Au through a hydrothermal reaction, and finally, they pyrolyzed the resulting material at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ catalyst. In comparison with the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}$ counterparts, the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ catalyst exhibited a remarkably high catalytic activity toward the reduction of 4- nitrophenol to 4-aminophenol by $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4},}$ with a stable conversion of $87.9\%$ for at least three additional cycles.
